Title:
EDR Introspection (EDR-Introspection.pdf)

Type:
Research Paper

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- This paper analyzes how Endpoint Detection & Response (EDR) products instrument Windows systems and how their telemetry/controls can be observed (“introspected”) from the host.  
- It focuses on identifying EDR components and monitoring points (user-mode and kernel-mode) to understand what an EDR can see and where it enforces policy.  
- The work discusses practical ways to enumerate and characterize EDR presence via artifacts such as drivers, services, callbacks/hooks, ETW providers/consumers, and protected processes.  
- It is useful for red teams and malware researchers to map detection surfaces and for blue teams to validate coverage, hardening, and tamper-resistance assumptions.  
- The key value is a structured methodology for reasoning about EDR visibility and attack surface rather than relying on vendor claims or black-box testing.

Technical Focus:
- Windows EDR architecture (user-mode agents + kernel drivers)
- ETW telemetry (providers, sessions, consumers)
- Kernel instrumentation (callbacks, minifilters, object/process/thread notify routines)
- Process protection and tamper resistance (PPL/PP, protected services/drivers)
- Artifact-based EDR identification (services, drivers, registry, named objects)

Use Cases:
- Build an “EDR fingerprinting” checklist for engagements and lab validation
- Assess which Windows events/operations are likely monitored on a target host
- Compare EDR products by observable instrumentation points and coverage
- Identify potential blind spots or high-signal telemetry sources for detection engineering
- Support threat modeling of EDR bypass/tamper attempts (from an introspection perspective)

Keywords:
Windows EDR, introspection, ETW, kernel driver, minifilter, PsSetCreateProcessNotifyRoutine, ObRegisterCallbacks, registry artifacts, services, callbacks, hooks, telemetry, PPL, Protected Process Light, tamper protection, Sysmon, AMSI, user-mode agent, kernel-mode monitoring